{
  "id": "switch-panel",
  "title": "Switch Panel",
  "arc": 1,
  "stages": [
    {
      "type": "briefing",
      "lines": [
        "Rover needs backup lights. If one bulb dies mid-mission, the other must stay on.",
        "That means choosing how to wire two bulbs. There are two ways, and they behave very differently."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "toy": {
        "kind": "series-parallel",
        "prompt": "Switch between the two ways and read what changes."
      },
      "chunks": {
        "1": [
          "Wire two bulbs one after the other and that is [[series]]. They share the push, so both are dim.",
          "Wire them side by side and that is [[parallel]]. Each gets the full push, so both are bright.",
          "Side by side is great for backup lights. But the [[battery]] works harder, so it runs out sooner."
        ],
        "2": [
          "In [[series]] the same [[current]] flows through both and the [[voltage]] is shared. In [[parallel]] each path gets the full [[voltage]] and the currents add.",
          "Equal bulbs in parallel: twice the current from the battery, so the battery drains twice as fast."
        ],
        "3": [
          "[[series]]: R_total = R1 + R2, one current, voltage divides. [[parallel]]: 1/R_total = 1/R1 + 1/R2, one voltage, currents add.",
          "Bulb power goes as V²/R. Half the voltage across each series bulb means a quarter of the power, so they are dim. And on a battery too big for one bulb, two in series can be exactly right."
        ]
      },
      "help": {
        "1": "Series is a single lane. Parallel is two lanes.",
        "2": "Traffic: one road with two toll booths in a row is slow (series). Two roads, one booth each, is fast (parallel).",
        "3": "Try both buttons and read the line of words under them.",
        "4": "Press series, then parallel. Notice: series is dim, parallel is bright."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "formula": "parallel-resistance",
      "intro": {
        "1": "Side by side, more can flow, so the total squeeze goes down.",
        "2": "Two paths side by side: the total [[resistance]] is smaller than either. Into the Formula Book.",
        "3": "1/R_total = 1/R1 + 1/R2, or (R1 × R2) ÷ (R1 + R2) for two. Into the Formula Book."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "example",
      "title": "Worked example",
      "steps": {
        "1": [
          "Two bulbs, each 20 Ω, side by side.",
          "Two the same share the job. The total is half.",
          "20 ÷ 2 = 10.",
          "Total squeeze: 10 Ω."
        ],
        "2": [
          "A 20 Ω path and a 30 Ω path side by side.",
          "Multiply them: 20 × 30 = 600.",
          "Add them: 20 + 30 = 50.",
          "Divide: 600 ÷ 50 = 12. Total: 12 Ω, smaller than both."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "try",
      "title": "Try one",
      "questions": [
        {
          "prompt": {
            "1": "Two {R} Ω paths side by side. What is the total squeeze, in ohms?",
            "2": "A {R1} Ω path and a {R2} Ω path side by side. What is the total squeeze, in ohms?",
            "3": "A {V} V battery feeds a {R1} Ω path and a {R2} Ω path in parallel. How many mA does the battery supply?"
          },
          "unit": {
            "1": "Ω",
            "2": "Ω",
            "3": "mA"
          },
          "gen": {
            "1": {
              "vars": {
                "R": {
                  "pick": [
                    10,
                    20,
                    40,
                    60
                  ]
                }
              },
              "answer": "R / 2"
            },
            "2": {
              "vars": {
                "R1": {
                  "pick": [
                    10,
                    15,
                    20,
                    30,
                    60
                  ]
                },
                "R2": {
                  "pick": [
                    10,
                    15,
                    20,
                    30,
                    60
                  ]
                }
              },
              "answer": "R1 * R2 / (R1 + R2)",
              "where": [
                "R1 * R2 / (R1 + R2) == round(R1 * R2 / (R1 + R2) * 10) / 10"
              ],
              "dp": 1
            },
            "3": {
              "vars": {
                "V": {
                  "pick": [
                    6,
                    9
                  ]
                },
                "R1": {
                  "pick": [
                    20,
                    30,
                    60
                  ]
                },
                "R2": {
                  "pick": [
                    20,
                    30,
                    60
                  ]
                }
              },
              "answer": "V / Rt * 1000",
              "derived": {
                "Rt": "R1 * R2 / (R1 + R2)"
              },
              "dp": 1
            }
          },
          "explain": {
            "1": "Two the same share the job, so the total is half: {R} ÷ 2 = {answer} Ω.",
            "2": "(R1 × R2) ÷ (R1 + R2) = ({R1} × {R2}) ÷ ({R1} + {R2}) = {answer} Ω.",
            "3": "R_total = ({R1} × {R2}) ÷ ({R1} + {R2}) = {Rt} Ω. I = {V} ÷ {Rt} = {answer} mA."
          },
          "wrong": [
            {
              "expr": "R / 1",
              "why": "Side by side makes the squeeze go DOWN, not stay the same."
            }
          ],
          "hints": [
            "Side by side means the total is smaller than either path.",
            "Two the same: halve it. Different: multiply, then divide by the sum.",
            "Use your Formula Book."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "help": {
        "1": "Side by side, the total goes down.",
        "2": "Two doors instead of one: more people get through, so the door squeeze is smaller.",
        "3": "Example: 20 Ω and 20 Ω in parallel is 10 Ω.",
        "4": "Watch: multiply your two numbers, add them, then divide. Try again."
      }
    },
